@page "/components/ai/streamingmarkdown"
@implements IAsyncDisposable

<PageOutlet Url="components/ai/streamingmarkdown"
            Title="AiStreamingMarkdown"
            Description="BitAiStreamingMarkdown renders Markdown that is still being written by an AI model: parsed incrementally, settled at every step - no flashing asterisks, no half-highlighted code." />

<DemoPage Name="AiStreamingMarkdown"
          Description="BitAiStreamingMarkdown renders Markdown that is still being written, as a BitAiMarkdownView that follows the text while it grows. It holds one incremental parser for its lifetime, so the text already seen is parsed once however many deltas arrive - a long streamed answer costs linear work rather than quadratic. While the text streams, a caret trails it, its last block stays open, a code block is highlighted only once its fence closes and a dangling emphasis or code delimiter is hidden until its partner arrives."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          SubClasses="componentSubClasses"
          GitHubAIUrl="StreamingMarkdown/BitAiStreamingMarkdown.razor"
          GitHubDemoUrl="AI/StreamingMarkdown/BitAiStreamingMarkdownDemo.razor">
    <NotesTemplate>
        <BitText>
            To use this component, you need to install the
            <BitLink Href="https://www.nuget.org/packages/Bit.BlazorUI.AI" Target="_blank">
                <BitTag Reversed
                        Text="Bit.BlazorUI.AI"
                        Color="BitColor.SecondaryBackground"
                        IconName="@BitIconName.NavigateExternalInline" />
            </BitLink>
            nuget package, as described in the Optional steps of the
            <BitLink Href="/getting-started">Getting started</BitLink> page.
        </BitText>
        <br />
        <BitText>
            It needs no chat session: hand it the text received so far. Inside a chat,
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/textpartview">AiTextPartView</BitLink> renders every text part with it, and the
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/reasoningpartview">reasoning</BitLink> of a message and a document
            <BitLink Href="/components/ai/artifactview">artifact</BitLink> use it too. Re-render it at a steady
            pace rather than on every token - a <b>BitAiRenderScheduler</b> (see below; the AI components use
            <b>BitAiUiOptions.RenderInterval</b>) collapses any number of deltas into one render per 50 ms, which on Blazor Server
            is what keeps the circuit quiet. Its <b>BitAiIncrementalMarkdownParser</b> (see below) is public too, for a view of
            your own that renders streamed Markdown with a BitAiMarkdownView. The parser is built from the Markdown options in force
            (<b>BitAiUiOptions.Markdown</b>, see <BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview">AiMarkdownView</BitLink>) and the
            output is untrusted: raw HTML is escaped and script URLs are neutralised. See it at work in the
            <BitLink Href="/ai/scenarios/streaming">Streaming</BitLink> scenario.
        </BitText>
    </NotesTemplate>
    <Examples>
        <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example1CsharpCode" Id="example1">
            <div>
                <b>Text</b> is the Markdown received so far. With <b>IsStreaming</b> left false the text is complete, and it renders
                like any Markdown view.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@basicMarkdown" />
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Streaming" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example2CsharpCode" CodeFiles="@example2CodeFiles" Id="example2">
            <div>
                While <b>IsStreaming</b> is true more text is expected: a caret trails it and its last block is left open. Stream
                this answer and watch the raw text beside what is drawn: the dangling <b>**</b> of a bold run never shows, a table
                fills in row by row, the code block stays plain until its closing fence arrives and is highlighted then. Turn on
                <b>Slow motion</b> to follow every step. The page re-renders through a <b>BitAiRenderScheduler</b>, at most once per
                50 ms.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitStack Horizontal Wrap AutoHeight Gap="0.5rem" VerticalAlign="BitAlignment.Center">
                <BitButton OnClick="StreamAsync" Disabled="isStreaming" IconName="@BitIconName.Play">Stream the answer</BitButton>
                <BitButton OnClick="StopStreaming" Disabled="isStreaming is false" IconName="@BitIconName.Stop" Variant="BitVariant.Outline">Stop</BitButton>
                <BitToggle @bind-Value="slowMotion" Text="Slow motion" />
            </BitStack>
            <br />
            <div class="stream-grid">
                <div>
                    <div class="stream-title">Received so far</div>
                    <pre class="stream-raw">@streamedText</pre>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <div class="stream-title">Rendered</div>
                    <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@streamedText" IsStreaming="isStreaming" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Citations" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example3CsharpCode" Id="example3">
            <div>
                A bracketed number the model writes (<b>[1]</b>) becomes a citation chip when <b>Citations</b> holds a citation of
                that <b>Number</b>; one that matches none stays the text it was. <b>OnCitationClick</b> is raised when the user
                activates a chip.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@citedMarkdown"
                                    Citations="citations"
                                    OnCitationClick="(BitAiCitation citation) => clickedSource = citation.Source.Title" />
            <br />
            <div>Activated: <b>@(clickedSource ?? "none yet")</b></div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Markdown options" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example4CsharpCode" Id="example4">
            <div>
                The parser follows <b>BitAiUiOptions.Markdown</b> (a <BitLink Href="/components/ai/markdownview#markdown-options">BitAiMarkdownOptions</BitLink>),
                and is built again when a different options instance is cascaded, so the parser and the renderer always agree.
                Chat-tuned models often write one line per sentence and mean the line breaks: <b>SoftBreaksAsHardBreaks</b> keeps
                them (the first text). <b>HideDanglingDelimiters</b>, on by default, keeps the opening <b>**</b> of a bold run that
                has not closed yet out of sight while the text streams (the second text, frozen mid-word with <b>IsStreaming</b>);
                turned off, the asterisks flash on the way in. Make a new options instance only when a choice changes and keep it
                in a field - a new one on every render would rebuild the parser every time.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="keepLineBreaks" @bind-Value:after="UpdateMarkdownOptions" Text="SoftBreaksAsHardBreaks" />
            <BitToggle @bind-Value="hideDanglingDelimiters" @bind-Value:after="UpdateMarkdownOptions" Text="HideDanglingDelimiters" />
            <BitAiOptionsProvider UiOptions="markdownOptions">
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@lineBreakMarkdown" />
                <br />
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@danglingMarkdown" IsStreaming />
            </BitAiOptionsProvider>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example5CsharpCode" Id="example5">
            <div>
                <b>BitParams</b> with a <b>BitAiStreamingMarkdownParams</b> sets the look of every streaming Markdown inside it - the
                text parts of a chat included - here the headings of its <b>Markdown</b> view in the primary color. A parameter of
                the component itself still wins, and wins whole: the second sets <b>Styles</b> of its own, which take the place of
                the cascaded ones.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitParams Parameters="streamingParams">
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown" />
                <br />
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown" Styles="@(new() { Markdown = new() { ListItem = "font-style: italic;" } })" />
            </BitParams>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example6CsharpCode" Id="example6">
            <div>
                <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> reach the root, <b>Styles</b> and <b>Classes</b> its parts: <b>Markdown</b> takes the
                classes and styles of every part of the Markdown view the text is drawn with. The streaming Markdown publishes no CSS
                variables of its own: the <b>--bit-AiMarkdownView-*</b> variables restyle it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="example-content">
                @* A plain element of this page for the scoped ::deep rules of the custom classes to anchor on. *@
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown" Style="padding: 0.5rem; border: 1px dashed dodgerblue;" />
                <br />
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown" Class="custom-class" />
                <br />
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown"
                                        Styles="@(new() { Markdown = new() { Heading = "color: tomato;", ListItem = "font-style: italic;" } })" />
                <br />
                <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown"
                                        Classes="@(new() { Root = "custom-root", Markdown = new() { Heading = "custom-heading" } })" />
                <br />
                <div style="--bit-AiMarkdownView-heading-color: mediumseagreen; --bit-AiMarkdownView-marker-color: mediumseagreen; --bit-AiMarkdownView-line-height: 2;">
                    <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Text="@styleMarkdown" />
                </div>
            </div>
        </DemoExample>

        <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example7CsharpCode" Id="example7">
            <div>
                In right-to-left text the Markdown reads from the right and the caret trails the text at its left end, whether the
                direction comes from <b>Dir</b> or from a <b>dir</b> attribute around it.
            </div>
            <br />
            <BitAiStreamingMarkdown Dir="BitDir.Rtl" Text="@rtlMarkdown" IsStreaming />
        </DemoExample>
    </Examples>
</DemoPage>
